  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1115 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe flow aloft will increase on Friday ahead of another trough\naxis. This also looks to be the last warm day before a cooler\npattern takes over for the weekend into next week, with highs\nreaching the upper 50s to low 60s. 700mb flow reaches about 40kt \nby midday Friday in a broadly subsident regime, meaning that \nwidespread wind gusts are expected to develop across the Front \nRange and plains during the afternoon. For the lower elevations, \nelevated fire weather conditions will again be a concern in this \npattern. Two limiting factors are (1) the northern I-25 corridor \noften ends up at least partially sheltered in SW flow, and (2) \nrelative humidity looks to be at least a little bit higher than \nWednesday.\n\nAcross the mountains, moisture will begin to increase during the\nmorning hours, leading to a gradual increase in snow. With a \n~120kt jet streak remaining nearly stationary over northwestern \nColorado, one dominant band of light (to at times moderate) snow \nsomewhere over northern Colorado, with weaker orographic snow \nshowers across the rest of the mountains. Overall moisture is \nstill somewhat limited, and there may be a break in between \nshortwaves Friday afternoon into Friday night.\n\nA second shortwave is expected to track through the state on\nSaturday. There's enough moisture for another organized round of\nlight snow, this time focused along and south of the I-70\nmountains. Snowfall totals in this period will again be on the\nlight side. Guidance continues to struggle with the evolution of\nthis shortwave as is tracks into southwestern Kansas during the\nday. Several deterministic models (e.g., ECMWF, RGEM) are \ninsistent that a band of snow will spread into the Denver metro \nand/or Palmer Divide during the morning hours Saturday, with \ngenerally light (roughly a half inch to inch) snowfall amounts \npossible. On the other hand, the GFS and most of the American\nshort term/regional guidance is weaker with the developing surface\nlow over the OK panhandle and struggle to produce any QPF outside\nof the higher terrain. I have a slight preference towards the\nECMWF solution in this time period and our grids are close to\nthe previous forecast - which is higher than the current NBM.\n\nThis system will also usher in a period of cooler weather across \nthe forecast area. Even between systems Sunday and Monday, \ntemperatures across the plains will likely settle in the mid to \nupper 40s. Guidance is in good agreement that near to below normal\ntemperatures will continue through the rest of the forecast \nperiod with highs sticking in the 40s.\n\nA fairly active pattern also looks likely to continue next week,\nwith trough working through the region every few days. This means\nthere's chance for additional mountain snow Monday into Tuesday,\nand again sometime late week next week. What system brings the\nbest chance at snow to the region? It's difficult to say.\nDeterministic solutions continue to swing wildly from run to run\nin this period. As we've seen with this upcoming Friday/Saturday\nsystem, this pattern continues to have low predictability, and\nlarge day-to-day/run-to-run swings are likely to continue through\nmost of the upcoming forecast period.",
